No macroscopic object is ever truly isolated; it is constantly bombarded by trillions of photons of light, air molecules, and thermal vibrations. Zurek showed that this environment does not merely destroy quantum coherence—it curates and broadcasts information through an evolutionary-style selection process:

Step 1: Einselection (Environment-Induced Superselection)

• When an object interacts with its surrounding environment, almost all fragile quantum superpositions are rapidly scrambled and destroyed (decoherence).
• Only a tiny subset of ultra-stable states—called pointer states (such as a well-defined spatial position)—survive the bombardment intact without getting scrambled. These are the "fittest" quantum states that survive natural selection by the environment.

